/*
  core/adversary.spthy -- ADVERSARY-INDUCED STRESSORS (ROADMAP #6).

  The Dolev-Yao adversary does not only act on the wire; it can DRIVE the human into a degraded mask by
  MANUFACTURING a stressor condition, then exploit the degradation. This is the dual of a first-class
  interface (ROADMAP #2): a good interface LOWERS a step's demand to prevent a stressor; the adversary
  RAISES it to induce one.

  Capability modelled here: INJECT URGENCY. The attacker plants a fake "act now or lose access" lure on a
  step it controls, marking that step !Urgent. The adversary-induced TimePressure detector
  (core/stressors/time_pressure_induced.spthy) reads !Urgent to raise the effective temporal demand ->
  Busy, regardless of the step's base (lexicon) demand. Gated by In(pid): the adversary can only target a
  step whose id it knows -- i.e. one it injected itself (e.g. a prompt-bombed approval).

  This makes the stressor an ATTACK VECTOR, not just an environmental hazard, and the AdvInduced marker
  lets attribution lemmas prove a degradation traces to the adversary -- identifying which stressors an
  RFC must defend (number-matching MFA / shutout defeats the resulting auto-approval; see P6 / P8).
*/

rule Adv_inject_urgency:
    [ In(pid) ]
  --[ InjectUrgency(pid), AdvInduced('TimePressure') ]->
    [ !Urgent(pid) ]
